---
<Base
  title="About Red River Gorge Hiker"
  description="About Red River Gorge Hiker, a Kentucky Red River Gorge brand rooted in hiking, photography, stories, gear, and support for Wolfe County Search & Rescue."
>
  <div class="page prose about-copy">
    <h1>About Red River Gorge Hiker</h1>
    <p class="eyebrow">For the people who hike it, love it, and keep coming back.</p>

    <p>Red River Gorge Hiker is about a connection to a place - the trails, ridgelines, sandstone, waterfalls, campsites, changing weather, long days outside, and the experiences that keep drawing people back to Kentucky’s Red River Gorge.</p>

    <p>Red River Gorge Hiker is operated by Red River Gorge Hiker, LLC.</p>

    <p>It began with one hiker spending a lot of time exploring that place, but it was never meant to end with him.</p>

    <p><strong>He didn’t begin exploring the Gorge in search of photographs. The photographs came from exploring it.</strong></p>

    <p>That distinction still matters. Red River Gorge Hiker isn't a photography business that happened to choose the Gorge as its subject. It grew from hiking it, camping in it, getting to know it, and wanting to share some of what makes it worth returning to.</p>

    <hr />

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">The Gorge is the point</p>
      <h2>More than the photographs.</h2>

      <p>Photography was where Red River Gorge Hiker began, but the idea has grown.</p>

      <p>Today, RRGH brings together original photography, stories from the trail, hiking and backcountry information, puzzles and gear inspired by the Gorge, and a growing commitment to the people and organizations connected to this place.</p>

      <p>The common thread is simple: <strong>the Red River Gorge comes first.</strong></p>

      <p>The photographs mean more because of where they came from. The stories matter because they were lived there. The gear means something because Red River Gorge Hiker is meant to be a name people who share that connection can recognize as their own.</p>
    </section>

    <hr />

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">Giving something back</p>
      <h2>Supporting Wolfe County Search &amp; Rescue</h2>

      <p>Loving the Gorge also means recognizing the people who answer the call when a day in the woods goes wrong.</p>

      <p>Wolfe County Search &amp; Rescue serves people exploring some of the same trails, cliffs, ridges, hollows, and backcountry that Red River Gorge Hiker celebrates.</p>

      <p>Supporting their work has become part of what RRGH is about.</p>

      <p>Red River Gorge Hiker makes direct contributions to Wolfe County Search &amp; Rescue, helping support the team and the work they do for the people who come here to hike, climb, camp, explore, and experience the Gorge.</p>

      <p>It's one way a business built around this place can give something back to it.</p>

      <p><strong><a href={`${base}search-and-rescue/`}>Learn more about RRGH and Wolfe County Search &amp; Rescue →</a></strong></p>
    </section>

    <hr />

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">How it started</p>
      <h2>From photography to something broader.</h2>
      <p>Red River Gorge Hiker grew from years spent hiking, backpacking, photographing, and exploring Kentucky’s Red River Gorge and Clifty Wilderness.</p>
      <p>What began with original photography has grown into something broader: a place for art, stories, gear, useful information, local relationships, and selected work connected to people who genuinely know the Gorge.</p>
      <p>The goal is not to make one person the center of the story. It is to celebrate the landscape, the experiences people carry home from it, and the people who explore, document, create, and serve here.</p>
      <p><strong>For the people who know the Gorge.</strong></p>
    </section>

    <hr />

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">Red River Gorge Hiker</p>
      <h2>For the people who know the Gorge.</h2>

      <p>It’s for the person heading down the trail early in the morning. The backpacker setting up for another night in the woods. The person seeing the Gorge for the first time. The one coming back to a favorite trail, overlook, arch, or waterfall.</p>

      <p>Red River Gorge Hiker is for people who experience this place in their own way and carry some part of it with them when they leave.</p>

      <p>You don't have to take photographs.</p>

      <p>You just have to understand why this place gets into you - and why you keep coming back.</p>

      <p><strong>For the people who hike it, love it, and keep coming back.</strong></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
